    <section class="section" id="s1">
      <h2>1. Introduction: The New Digital Frontier</h2>
      <p>Imagine you are a detective investigating a crime. In the past, you would examine physical evidence —
        fingerprints, footprints, DNA. Today, crimes increasingly happen in the <strong>digital world</strong>: stolen
        data, hijacked networks, forged documents, and ransomware attacks. The investigators of tomorrow need entirely
        new tools — and that is exactly where <strong>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</strong>, <strong>Virtual Reality
          (VR)</strong>, and <strong>Augmented Reality (AR)</strong> come in.</p>
      <p>These three technologies are not science fiction. They are being actively deployed by governments, law
        enforcement agencies, corporations, and universities right now to fight cybercrime and solve digital cases
        faster, smarter, and with greater accuracy than ever before.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">🔍 Quick Analogy</div>
        Think of <strong>AI</strong> as a super-intelligent forensic analyst that never sleeps and can read a million
        logs in a second. Think of <strong>VR</strong> as a crime-scene reconstruction room where investigators walk
        through a crime without touching any real evidence. And think of <strong>AR</strong> as smart glasses that
        overlay digital clues onto the physical world in real-time.
      </div>

      <h3>Why Do These Technologies Matter?</h3>
      <p>Cybercrime is growing at an alarming pace. Traditional methods of detecting intrusions, analysing digital
        evidence, and training security professionals simply cannot keep up. AI, VR, and AR address this gap in three
        important ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Speed:</strong> AI can analyse millions of data points per second — far beyond human capability.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Machine learning models detect patterns invisible to the human eye.</li>
        <li><strong>Immersion:</strong> VR and AR allow security professionals to train, investigate, and present
          evidence in interactive three-dimensional environments.</li>
      </ul>

