    <section class="section" id="s2">
      <h2>2. Artificial Intelligence in Cybersecurity</h2>

      <h3>What Is AI?</h3>
      <p><strong>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</strong> refers to computer systems that can perform tasks requiring
        human-like intelligence — recognising patterns, making decisions, understanding language, and learning from
        experience. In cybersecurity, AI acts like a highly trained analyst monitoring every corner of a network
        simultaneously, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, without ever getting tired or missing a clue.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">📘 Key Concept — Machine Learning</div>
        A subset of AI called <strong>Machine Learning (ML)</strong> allows systems to learn from data without being
        explicitly programmed. Instead of writing rules like "block this IP address," you feed the system millions of
        examples of normal and abnormal network traffic — and it learns to tell the difference on its own. Over time, it
        becomes more accurate as it sees more examples.
      </div>

      <h3>How AI Works in Cybersecurity — Step by Step</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-content"><strong>Data Collection</strong><span>The system continuously collects data —
              network packets, login attempts, file changes, emails, and system logs — from across the entire
              organisation.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-content"><strong>Baseline Learning</strong><span>The AI learns what "normal" looks like. A
              user who logs in from Mumbai at 9 AM on weekdays is normal. A login from an unknown country at 3 AM is
              suspicious.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-content"><strong>Anomaly Detection</strong><span>When something deviates from the baseline,
              the AI flags it as a potential threat, assigns a risk score, and alerts the security team.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-content"><strong>Automated Response</strong><span>In advanced systems, the AI does not just
              alert — it acts. It can block an IP, quarantine a device, lock an account, or isolate a network segment
              automatically.</span></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="step-num">5</div>
          <div class="step-content"><strong>Continuous Learning</strong><span>The AI updates its models after every
              incident, becoming smarter and more accurate over time — like a detective who improves with every case
              they solve.</span></div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Core AI Techniques Used in Cybersecurity</h3>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="mini-card">
          <div class="mini-card-icon">🧠</div>
          <h4>Neural Networks</h4>
          <p>Modelled after the human brain. They learn to classify threats by processing layers of data — widely used
            in malware detection and spam filtering.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
          <div class="mini-card-icon">🌲</div>
          <h4>Random Forests</h4>
          <p>Combines many decision trees to classify data as benign or malicious. Highly effective for intrusion
            detection systems (IDS).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
          <div class="mini-card-icon">🔄</div>
          <h4>Recurrent Neural Nets</h4>
          <p>Designed for sequential data like network logs. They remember past events to detect attacks that unfold
            slowly over time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
          <div class="mini-card-icon">📊</div>
          <h4>Clustering Algorithms</h4>
          <p>Group similar data points together. Used to discover previously unknown malware families by clustering
            similar code behaviours.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
          <div class="mini-card-icon">🗣️</div>
          <h4>NLP</h4>
          <p>Analyses text — emails, chat logs, social media — to detect phishing, social engineering attempts, and
            insider threats using language patterns.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
          <div class="mini-card-icon">🎭</div>
          <h4>Generative AI / GANs</h4>
          <p>Creates realistic attack simulations for testing defences. Also generates synthetic training data for rare
            or novel attack types.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

