    <section class="section" id="s3">
      <h2>3. Types &amp; Applications of AI in Cybersecurity</h2>

      <h3>3.1 Threat Detection &amp; Prevention</h3>
      <p>This is AI's most critical role. Traditional antivirus software works by matching files against a database of
        known malware signatures — but new malware is created every day, and novel threats have no signature to match.
        AI solves this with <strong>behavioural analysis</strong>: instead of looking for a known face, it looks for
        suspicious behaviour, even in entirely new and unseen threats.</p>

      <div class="callout amber">
        <div class="callout-title">⚡ Real-World Example</div>
        <strong>Darktrace</strong>, a UK-based cybersecurity firm, uses AI modelled on the human immune system. It
        monitors enterprise networks and can identify self-replicating ransomware spreading through a network within
        seconds — and autonomously quarantine it before human operators even receive an alert.
      </div>

      <h3>3.2 Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) &amp; SIEM</h3>
      <p>A <strong>Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)</strong> system collects logs from every device in a
        network and uses AI to correlate events and detect multi-stage attacks. For example, an attacker might:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>First conduct a port scan (reconnaissance)</li>
        <li>Then attempt brute-force login (initial access)</li>
        <li>Then escalate privileges quietly (lateral movement)</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Each step alone might look harmless. AI-powered SIEM connects these dots and raises a high-severity alert even
        when each individual event was below the normal threshold of concern.</p>

      <h3>3.3 Phishing &amp; Social Engineering Detection</h3>
      <p>Phishing — tricking people into giving up credentials by impersonating a trustworthy entity — accounts for over
        80% of security incidents globally. AI-powered email filters use NLP to analyse sender reputation, email body
        language (urgency cues, suspicious links, grammatical irregularities), and behavioural metadata (time of send,
        bulk delivery patterns) to flag and quarantine phishing attempts before they reach the inbox.</p>

      <h3>3.4 Automated Penetration Testing</h3>
      <p>Penetration testing involves ethically hacking your own systems to find vulnerabilities before attackers do. AI
        tools can automatically discover attack surfaces, generate exploit strategies, and prioritise vulnerabilities by
        severity — compressing what was once a week-long manual engagement into hours of automated scanning and
        analysis.</p>

      <h3>3.5 Insider Threat Detection</h3>
      <p>Not all threats come from outside. Disgruntled employees, compromised accounts, or negligent users pose serious
        risks from within. AI builds a <strong>User and Entity Behaviour Analytics (UEBA)</strong> profile for each user
        — flagging when someone suddenly downloads 10,000 customer records, accesses systems outside their role, or
        connects from an unfamiliar device at an unusual hour.</p>

