    <section class="section" id="s4">
      <h2>4. AI in Digital Forensics</h2>

      <p>Digital forensics is the science of recovering, preserving, and analysing digital evidence to investigate
        crimes and security incidents. It is like archaeology — instead of digging through soil, investigators dig
        through hard drives, cloud accounts, network logs, and encrypted files. AI is transforming this field
        dramatically, enabling investigators to process evidence that would otherwise take months in a matter of hours.
      </p>

      <h3>4.1 Automated Evidence Collection &amp; Triage</h3>
      <p>A single investigation might involve terabytes of data — thousands of emails, documents, images, and system
        logs. Manually reviewing all of this is impossible within reasonable timeframes. AI tools automatically triage
        digital evidence: classifying documents by relevance, detecting duplicates, extracting metadata, and surfacing
        the most critical artefacts first.</p>

      <div class="callout green">
        <div class="callout-title">🕵️ Forensic Analogy</div>
        Think of AI triage like a smart librarian. Instead of reading every book in a library of 10,000 volumes to find
        what is relevant to your case, the librarian instantly pulls out the 20 most relevant books and highlights the
        key passages. The investigator reads those 20 books intelligently — not all 10,000.
      </div>

      <h3>4.2 Image &amp; Video Analysis</h3>
      <p>AI-powered image recognition can scan thousands of photos or video frames in minutes to identify faces,
        objects, locations, and text. In child exploitation investigations, tools use hash-matching AI to detect known
        illegal imagery. In fraud cases, AI reads receipts, handwriting, and document metadata. In national security
        investigations, facial recognition identifies suspects across hours of surveillance footage in seconds.</p>

      <h3>4.3 Malware Analysis &amp; Reverse Engineering</h3>
      <p>When investigators encounter an unknown piece of malware, understanding how it works is crucial. AI systems
        analyse malware binaries by examining their structure, code patterns, system calls, and behavioural signatures —
        automatically classifying them into known families (ransomware, trojan, worm, spyware) and predicting their
        capabilities without requiring time-consuming manual reverse engineering of every line of code.</p>

      <h3>4.4 Timeline Reconstruction</h3>
      <p>Establishing a <strong>timeline</strong> of events is one of the most important tasks in forensics — what
        happened, in what order, and when. AI correlates timestamps from multiple sources (file system, event logs,
        emails, browser history, GPS data) to automatically reconstruct a chronological narrative, helping investigators
        understand exactly what an attacker or suspect did and in what sequence.</p>

      <h3>4.5 Natural Language Processing in Forensics</h3>
      <p>Investigators frequently need to review thousands of emails, messages, and documents. NLP tools can summarise
        large communication volumes, identify key entities (names, places, dates, financial amounts), detect emotional
        sentiment that may be relevant to a case, translate foreign-language content automatically, and cluster
        communications by topic for efficient review.</p>

      <div class="case-study">
        <div class="case-study-label">Case Study</div>
        <h4>Operation Tovar — Takedown of the Gameover Zeus Botnet (2014)</h4>
        <p>Gameover Zeus was a sophisticated banking trojan controlling a botnet of over 500,000 infected computers,
          stealing hundreds of millions of dollars globally. Law enforcement from over 10 countries collaborated.
          AI-assisted log analysis and network traffic correlation were critical in mapping the botnet's
          command-and-control infrastructure across thousands of servers worldwide.</p>
        <p><strong>Key AI role:</strong> Automated analysis of millions of DNS requests and network flows to map the
          distributed botnet infrastructure — enabling coordinated simultaneous server seizures across multiple
          jurisdictions.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

