    <section class="section" id="s5">
      <h2>5. Virtual Reality (VR) in Cybersecurity</h2>

      <h3>What Is Virtual Reality?</h3>
      <p><strong>Virtual Reality (VR)</strong> creates a fully immersive, computer-generated environment that users can
        explore and interact with using a headset and controllers. Unlike watching a video, VR places you
        <em>inside</em> the experience — you can look in any direction, move around, and interact with virtual objects
        as if they were real. Think of it as the ultimate simulation room.</p>

      <div class="callout purple">
        <div class="callout-title">🎮 Simple Analogy</div>
        If traditional cybersecurity training is reading a book about swimming, <strong>VR training is jumping into a
          virtual swimming pool</strong>. You practise the actual strokes, feel the simulated resistance, and learn
        through experience — not instruction. The muscle memory (and mental memory) formed is far stronger.
      </div>

      <h3>5.1 Cybersecurity Training Environments</h3>
      <p>Security professionals need to practise responding to cyberattacks — but you cannot practise on live production
        systems without catastrophic risk. VR solves this by creating <strong>realistic virtual networks</strong> where
        trainees experience real attack scenarios without any real-world consequences:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Simulated phishing attacks</strong> — trainees receive fake phishing emails in VR and practise
          identifying them under time pressure</li>
        <li><strong>Incident response drills</strong> — teams practise responding to a ransomware outbreak spreading
          through a virtual corporate network</li>
        <li><strong>Social engineering simulations</strong> — VR recreates a fake help-desk call scenario where trainees
          learn to resist manipulation tactics</li>
        <li><strong>Red team vs blue team exercises</strong> — attackers and defenders compete in a shared virtual
          environment with measurable outcomes</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>5.2 Visualising Network Topology in 3D</h3>
      <p>Networks can contain thousands of devices, connections, and data flows — nearly impossible to visualise
        meaningfully on a flat screen. VR allows security analysts to <strong>walk through their network</strong> in
        three dimensions, seeing every device, connection, and anomaly as physical objects in space. Suspicious
        connections glow red; healthy ones glow green. Analysts can literally reach out and interact with a compromised
        node to investigate it.</p>

