    <section class="section" id="s6">
      <h2>6. Augmented Reality (AR) in Cybersecurity</h2>

      <h3>What Is Augmented Reality?</h3>
      <p><strong>Augmented Reality (AR)</strong> overlays digital information onto the real physical world, typically
        through a smartphone camera, smart glasses, or a dedicated headset. Unlike VR, which replaces your surroundings
        entirely, AR <em>enhances</em> them. Think of it as a transparent heads-up display (HUD) showing you extra
        information about the real world in front of you.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">🔎 Everyday AR Example</div>
        When you use <strong>Google Maps Live View</strong> and see arrows overlaid on your camera view pointing which
        way to turn — that is augmented reality. Now imagine the same concept applied to a cybersecurity analyst
        examining a server room, or a forensic investigator walking through a crime scene, seeing device metadata and
        evidence labels floating in the air around them.
      </div>

      <h3>6.1 AR in Security Operations Centres (SOC)</h3>
      <p>Security Operations Centres monitor and respond to cyber threats in real-time. AR transforms the SOC experience
        in meaningful ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Smart displays</strong> — analysts wear AR glasses overlaying real-time threat metrics over their
          physical workspace</li>
        <li><strong>Contextual alerts</strong> — walking up to a physical server, an analyst's AR glasses display its
          current security status, open ports, recent alerts, and patch level</li>
        <li><strong>Collaborative investigation</strong> — multiple analysts in different geographic locations see the
          same AR workspace and collaborate on a case simultaneously</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>6.2 AR-Assisted Physical Security</h3>
      <p>Security guards and field investigators using AR-enabled devices can scan a person's face and receive instant
        identity verification, point their device at a server rack to see which machines are flagged as compromised,
        receive step-by-step forensic imaging instructions overlaid on equipment, and receive real-time geofencing
        alerts when a device leaves an authorised zone.</p>

      <h3>6.3 AR in Malware Visualisation</h3>
      <p>Malware analysts have traditionally worked with raw code and static reports. AR systems can render malware
        behaviour as interactive 3D graphs in the physical workspace — showing how ransomware encrypts files, which
        registry keys it modifies, and what network connections it attempts. This makes complex malware behaviour
        immediately comprehensible even to non-technical stakeholders — a capability invaluable in briefings for
        executives, boards, or law enforcement leadership.</p>

      <h3>6.4 AR for Real-Time Threat Detection in the Field</h3>
      <p>Law enforcement officers investigating a physical cybercrime scene — raiding a hacker's premises, for example —
        can use AR glasses to identify which devices in the room are powered on or recently used, detect wireless
        networks and rogue access points in the environment, tag and catalogue physical evidence in real-time for
        digital chain-of-custody records, and receive live instructions from a remote forensic expert who sees exactly
        what the officer sees.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 7 -->
    <section class="section" id="s7">
      <h2>7. VR &amp; AR in Digital Forensics</h2>

      <h3>7.1 Virtual Crime Scene Reconstruction</h3>
      <p>One of the most powerful applications of VR in forensics is the ability to <strong>reconstruct crime
          scenes</strong> from digital evidence. Investigators collect data from a scene — photographs, 3D scans, GPS
        coordinates, network logs — and feed it into VR software that creates a fully explorable virtual replica of the
        scene as it existed at the time of the crime. Investigators, lawyers, and judges can then walk through that
        scene on demand.</p>

      <div class="callout green">
        <div class="callout-title">🏛️ Courtroom Application</div>
        In a complex fraud trial involving computer evidence, a jury can wear VR headsets and walk through a
        <strong>virtual reconstruction</strong> of the suspect's office — seeing exactly where the devices were, what
        was displayed on each screen, and how the network was configured — making complex digital evidence immediately
        tangible and understandable to non-technical jurors.
      </div>

      <h3>7.2 Timeline Visualisation in VR</h3>
      <p>Digital forensic timelines can contain thousands of events spanning days, weeks, or months. VR allows
        investigators to experience the timeline as a <strong>three-dimensional river of time</strong> — walking forward
        through the chronological flow of events, zooming in on critical moments, branching off to examine related
        artefacts. This spatial representation of time dramatically improves comprehension compared to a flat
        spreadsheet of timestamps.</p>

      <h3>7.3 AR Evidence Tagging in the Field</h3>
      <p>When forensic investigators arrive at a physical crime scene involving digital devices, AR glasses allow them
        to visually scan the environment and automatically identify all electronic devices, tag each device with a
        digital evidence label visible through the AR display, record the precise GPS coordinates and 3D position of
        each piece of evidence, and automatically log records with timestamps and geotags into the case management
        system — all without touching a keyboard.</p>

      <h3>7.4 Expert Witness Presentations</h3>
      <p>Explaining a complex cyberattack to a jury of non-technical citizens is notoriously difficult. VR and AR enable
        forensic experts to present evidence as interactive, immersive experiences. Instead of showing a flowchart on a
        screen, an expert witness can guide jurors through a virtual reconstruction of how an attacker penetrated a
        corporate network — step by step, in a form that can be genuinely understood and retained.</p>

      <div class="case-study">
        <div class="case-study-label">Case Study</div>
        <h4>Hospital Network — VR-Based Incident Response Training Programme</h4>
        <p>A large hospital IT security division implemented a VR-based training programme for its incident response
          team. Trainees entered a virtual replica of the hospital's network during a simulated ransomware attack,
          practising detection, containment, and eradication procedures under time pressure. After six months of regular
          VR training exercises, the team's mean-time-to-detect (MTTD) for ransomware dropped by 34% and
          mean-time-to-respond (MTTR) dropped by 47%.</p>
        <p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Immersive, realistic VR training produces measurable performance improvements
          over traditional classroom-based methods for high-stakes incident response scenarios.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

